    "content": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nSTEP 2: Generate 51 perturbed CsPbBr3 structures.\n\n- structure_000.cif: unperturbed primitive base cell (from seekpath reduction)\n- structure_001..050.cif: random atomic displacements + small random lattice strain\n\nDisplacement magnitude justification:\n  At room temperature (300 K), the mean-square displacement of a typical\n  atom in a perovskite is on the order of 0.01-0.05 A (from Debye-Waller\n  factors in XRD/neutron data). We use a Gaussian displacement with\n  sigma = 0.05 A, which is at the upper end of room-temperature thermal\n  vibrations for light atoms (Br) and conservative for heavy atoms (Cs, Pb).\n  This is chemically reasonable: it does not break bonds or cause\n  unphysical overlaps, but it does lower the symmetry from Pm-3m to P1,\n  which is the point of the perturbation.\n\nLattice strain: a small random strain tensor with components drawn from\n  a uniform distribution in [-0.01, 0.01] (i.e. up to 1% strain). This\n  mimics the kind of lattice distortion that would arise from thermal\n  expansion or epitaxial strain, without changing the overall cell shape\n  dramatically.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom ase.io import read, write\nfrom ase import Atoms\nimport os\n\n# Read the primitive base structure (already reduced via seekpath in STEP 1)\nbase = read('structures/CsPbBr3_primitive.cif')\nprint(f\"Base structure: {len(base)} atoms, symbols = {base.get_chemical_symbols()}\")\nprint(f\"Base cell:\\n{base.cell[:].tolist()}\")\n\n# INVARIANT 1: nat and ntyp must be computed from the actual Atoms object\nnat = len(base)\ntypes = set(base.get_chemical_symbols())\nntyp = len(types)\nprint(f\"nat = {nat}, ntyp = {ntyp} (elements: {sorted(types)})\")\n\n# Displacement parameters (justified in module docstring)\nDISPLACEMENT_SIGMA = 0.05  # Angstrom, Gaussian sigma for atomic displacements\nSTRAIN_MAX = 0.01          # Maximum strain component (1%)\n\n# Random seed for reproducibility\nnp.random.seed(42)\n\n# Ensure output directory exists\nos.makedirs('structures', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Write structure_000: unperturbed base\nwrite('structures/structure_000.cif', base)\nprint(\"Wrote structures/structure_000.cif (unperturbed)\")\n\n# Verify structure_000\ncheck = read('structures/structure_000.cif')\nassert len(check) == nat, f\"structure_000: expected {nat} atoms, got {len(check)}\"\nassert len(set(check.get_chemical_symbols())) == ntyp, \\\n    f\"structure_000: expected {ntyp} types, got {len(set(check.get_chemical_symbols()))}\"\nprint(\"  Verified: nat and ntyp match base\")\n\n# Generate perturbed structures 001-050\nfor i in range(1, 51):\n    # Start from a copy of the base structure\n    atoms = base.copy()\n\n    # Apply random lattice strain: strain tensor with components in [-STRAIN_MAX, STRAIN_MAX]\n    # The strain tensor is applied as: new_cell = (I + strain) @ old_cell\n    # This is a small affine transformation of the cell vectors.\n    strain = np.random.uniform(-STRAIN_MAX, STRAIN_MAX, size=(3, 3))\n    new_cell = (np.eye(3) + strain) @ atoms.cell[:]\n    atoms.cell = new_cell\n\n    # Apply random atomic displacements in Cartesian coordinates\n    # Displacements are drawn from a Gaussian with sigma = DISPLACEMENT_SIGMA\n    displacements = np.random.normal(0.0, DISPLACEMENT_SIGMA, size=(len(atoms), 3))\n    atoms.positions += displacements\n\n    # INVARIANT 1: assert nat/ntyp for this structure\n    assert len(atoms) == nat, \\\n        f\"structure_{i:03d}: expected {nat} atoms, got {len(atoms)}\"\n    assert len(set(atoms.get_chemical_symbols())) == ntyp, \\\n        f\"structure_{i:03d}: expected {ntyp} types, got {len(set(atoms.get_chemical_symbols()))}\"\n\n    # Write the perturbed structure\n    fname = f'structures/structure_{i:03d}.cif'\n    write(fname, atoms)\n    print(f\"Wrote {fname} (strain max = {np.max(np.abs(strain)):.4f}, \"\n          f\"max displacement = {np.max(np.abs(displacements)):.4f} A)\")\n\nprint(f\"\\nDone. Generated 51 structures (000-050) in structures/\")\nprint(f\"All structures have nat={nat}, ntyp={ntyp}\")\n"
}

