one are given. kspacing ( float ) – Generate a grid of k-points with this as the minimum distance,\nin A^-1 between them in reciprocal space. If set to None, kpts\nwill be used instead. kpts ( ( int , int , int ) , dict or np.ndarray ) – If kpts is a tuple (or list) of 3 integers, it is interpreted\nas the dimensions of a Monkhorst-Pack grid. If kpts is set to None , only the Γ-point will be included\nand QE will use routines optimized for Γ-point-only calculations.', 'title': 'ase.io.espresso.write_espresso_in', 'url': 'https://docs.ase-lib.org/ase/io/formatoptions.html#ase.io.espresso.write_espresso_in', 'source': 'ase', 'metadata': {'source': 'ase', 'module': 'ase.io.formatoptions', 'qualified_name': 'ase.io.espresso.write_espresso_in', 'kind': 'function', 'name': 'write_espresso_in', 'class': None, 'function': 'write_espresso_in', 'chunk_index': 2, 'num_chunks': 5}, 'score': -0.41520994901657104, 'vector_score': None, 'bm25_score': 6.631808941352021, 'rerank_score': -0.41520994901657104}, {'text': '[ase.io.onetep.write_onetep_in]\npseudopotentials ( dict ) – Behaviour depends on the type: keys are species name(s) their\nvalue are the pseudopotential file to use:\n{‘Na’: ‘Na.usp’, ‘Cl’: ‘Cl.usp’} pseudo_path ( str ) – Where to look for pseudopotential, correspond\nto the pseudo_path keyword of ONETEP. pseudo_suffix ( str ) – Suffix for the pseudopotential filename\nto look for, useful if you have multiple sets of\npseudopotentials in pseudo_path.', 'title': 'ase.io.onetep.write_onetep_in', 'url': 'https://docs.ase-lib.org/ase/io/formatoptions.html#ase.io.onetep.write_onetep_in', 'source': 'ase', 'metadata': {'source': 'ase', 'module': 'ase.io.formatoptions', 'qualified_name': 'ase.io.onetep.write_onetep_in', 'kind': 'function', 'name': 'write_onetep_in', 'class': None, 'function': 'write_onetep_in', 'chunk_index': 3, 'num_chunks': 4}, 'score': -5.11313533782959, 'vector_score': None, 'bm25_score': 8.1006944548786, 'rerank_score': -5.11313533782959}, {'text': '[ase.io.extxyz.read_extxyz]\nIt’s easiest to describe the format with an example. Here is a\nstandard XYZ file containing a bulk cubic 8 atom silicon cell\n\n```\n8\n\nCubic\n\nbulk\n\nsilicon\n\ncell\n\nSi\n\n0.00000000\n\n0.00000000\n\n0.00000000\n\nSi\n\n1.36000000\n\n1.36000000\n\n1.36000000\n\nSi\n\n2.72000000\n\n2.72000000\n\n0.00000000\n\nSi\n\n4.08000000\n\n4.08000000\n\n1.36000000\n\nSi\n\n2.72000000\n\n0.00000000\n\n2.72000000\n\nSi\n\n4.08000000\n\n1.36000000\n\n4.08000000\n\nSi\n\n0.00000000\n\n2.72000000\n\n2.72000000\n\nSi\n\n1.36000000\n\n4.08000000\n\n4.08000000\n```\n\nThe first line is the number of atoms, followed by a comment and\nthen one line per atom, giving the element symbol and cartesian\nx y, and z coordinates in Angstroms.\n\nHere’s the same configuration in extended XYZ format\n\n```\n8\n\nLattice\n=\n"5.44 0.0 0.0 0.0 5.44 0.0 0.0 0.0 5.44"\n\nProperties\n=\nspecies\n:\nS\n:\n1\n:\npos\n:\nR\n:\n3\n\nTime\n=\n0.0\n\nSi\n\n0.00000000\n\n0.00000000\n\n0.00000000\n\nSi', 'title': 'ase.io.extxyz.read_extxyz', 'url': 'https://docs.ase-lib.org/ase/io/formatoptions.html#ase.io.extxyz.read_extxyz', 'source': 'ase', 'metadata': {'source': 'ase', 'module': 'ase.io.formatoptions', 'qualified_name': 'ase.io.extxyz.read_extxyz', 'kind': 'function', 'name': 'read_extxyz', 'class': None, 'function': 'read_extxyz', 'chunk_index': 1, 'num_chunks': 6}, 'score': -9.09611701965332, 'vector_score': 0.4654051661491394, 'bm25_score': None, 'rerank_score': -9.09611701965332}, {'text': '[ase.io.write]\nfilename: str or file Name of the file to write to or a file descriptor. The name ‘-’\nmeans standard output. images: Atoms object or list of Atoms objects A single Atoms object or a list of Atoms objects. format: str Used to specify the file-format. If not given, the\nfile-format will be taken from suffix of the filename. parallel: bool Default is to write on master only. Use parallel=False to write\nfrom all slaves. append: bool Default is to open files in ‘w’ or 